{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792799558,"nonce":"Z37gxTpWQJDhUVlMzrv30fWPl4E=","title":"Persist and resume local Jam documents across process exits","message":"Local follow-up to 2d4eb112e3bc802eb0c408d6092d2709fa5a8c9a32670a84766083a82e539a97 and recovery roadmap node 9bce95d3155bb5ebc1f9a0fc86336bce26d618cbb44dd8416cc95c71b5045119. Parent plan: 455de32231e2b4a39765b4dff0223570b525ccec145049431e6483f5aa521f98.\n\nImplement bounded, worker-owned local CRDT checkpoints and an update journal. Preserve document identity and CRDT clocks so a resumed host can merge a disconnected participant's edits. Persist accepted updates before host acknowledgement and local queued edits before reporting them locally saved. Keep invitations out of storage and rotate the secret when resuming.\n\nExpose explicit resume/rejoin actions for the active file. Do not automatically reconnect or write source files. Protect dirty buffers and reject recovery into an unrelated room even when filenames match. Ordinary per-author undo can restart after a process restart; full collaborative state must survive. Preserve corrupt checkpoints for diagnosis rather than silently overwriting them.\n\nVerify native storage bounds, lock ownership, incomplete journal tails, corrupt records and replay/compaction; kill real host/guest processes and resume them; run editor regressions and use the NUC as a second participant while quiet. Hosted durability, account identity, epoch compaction, asset/filesystem replication and full session history remain in the broader roadmap.","files":null}]}